@using LineOps.Desk.Windowing
@implements IDisposable
@inject WindowManager Manager
@inject DeskBrand Brand
@inject IJSRuntime JS
@inject IDeskAlerts Alerts

@*
    Fixed header: identity, the windows, and the desk's own controls.

    Two things live up here and they are not the same kind of thing, so they are not drawn
    the same way:

      · The windows — one strip, in its own recessed channel, spanning the bar. Every
        window is in it exactly once, as a key that is lit while its window is open.
        See WindowBar, which owns that rule.

      · The desk's own controls — workspaces and column widths. These act on the desk
        rather than being part of it, so they sit apart at the far end, after a hairline,
        as plain buttons. A second channel would have said "another strip of windows",
        which is what made the header read as two competing lists.

    The Window manager used to have a button here as well as its key in the strip: the same
    window, the same glyph, twice in one header. It has a key like everything else in the
    catalogue, and when it is open its key is lit like any other, so the button is gone.

    Destinations — a game, a team, a player — are about a subject and are reached by
    following a name in a panel, so they have no key here at all, open or not. Several can
    exist at once and their names are long, and appending them is what made the strip
    unreadable. They live where they already are, as columns on the desk.
*@

<header class="hdr">
    @* The brand is the workflow control. A workspace is a whole-desk arrangement — it
       replaces everything open — so it belongs to the desk rather than sitting in a cluster
       of per-window controls at the far end. Putting it on the identity says the same thing
       the arrangement does: this is the desk you are on, and this is how you change it. It
       also returns the width the Workspaces button was spending to the strip, which is the
       part of the header that actually has more to say. *@
    <RailMenu @ref="_workspaces" Class="hdr__brand-menu" Trigger="brand"
              Label="Workspaces — choose a desk arrangement">
        <div class="launcher" data-glide>
            <div class="launcher__group">Workspaces</div>
            @foreach (var workspace in Manager.Catalog.Workspaces)
            {
                <button class="launcher__item" type="button" role="menuitem"
                        @onclick="() => Apply(workspace)">
                    <Glyph Icon="@Icons.Material.Filled.Dashboard" />
                    <span>
                        @workspace.Name
                        <span class="launcher__desc">@workspace.Description</span>
                    </span>
                </button>
            }

            @if (Manager.UserWorkspaces.Count > 0)
            {
                <div class="launcher__group">Saved here</div>
                @foreach (var workspace in Manager.UserWorkspaces)
                {
                    <button class="launcher__item" type="button" role="menuitem"
                            @onclick="() => Apply(workspace)">
                        <Glyph Icon="@Icons.Material.Filled.Bookmark" />
                        <span>
                            @workspace.Name
                            <span class="launcher__desc">@workspace.Description</span>
                        </span>
                    </button>
                }
            }

            @* Where a Mac app keeps Settings… — under its name — with the chord beside it. *@
            <div class="launcher__group">Desk</div>
            <button class="launcher__item" type="button" role="menuitem"
                    aria-keyshortcuts="Control+Comma Meta+Comma" @onclick="OpenSettings">
                <Glyph Icon="@Icons.Material.Filled.Tune" />
                <span>Settings…<span class="launcher__desc">Ctrl+,</span></span>
            </button>

            <div class="launcher__group">Clear</div>
            <button class="launcher__item" type="button" role="menuitem" @onclick="CloseAll">
                <Glyph Icon="@Icons.Material.Filled.ClearAll" />
                <span>Close all<span class="launcher__desc">Empty the desk.</span></span>
            </button>
        </div>
    </RailMenu>

    @if (Manager.OpenCount == 0)
    {
        <span class="hdr__sub">@Brand.Tagline</span>
    }

    <div class="bar">
        <WindowBar />
    </div>

    <span class="hdr__divider" aria-hidden="true"></span>

    @* What is left of the desk's own controls: both are about the row as a whole. Back and
       Forward are not here any more: they moved onto each window's bar, where the direction
       they go is the window they sit on (AppWindow). The desk's own undo — bring back what was
       just closed — is the notice's Reopen key below, Ctrl+Z, and the browser's Back. *@
    <div class="hdr__tools" role="group" aria-label="Desk controls" data-glide>
        <button class="rail__btn" type="button" title="Go to anything (Ctrl+K)"
                aria-label="Go to anything" aria-keyshortcuts="Control+K Meta+K"
                @onclick="Manager.RequestPalette">
            <Glyph Icon="@Icons.Material.Filled.Search" />
        </button>

        <button class="rail__btn" type="button" title="Reset column widths"
                aria-label="Reset column widths"
                disabled="@(Manager.OpenCount == 0)"
                @onclick="Manager.ResetWidths">
            <Glyph Icon="@Icons.Material.Filled.ViewColumn" />
        </button>

        @* A window that vanished must say so: silent eviction reads as a bug, not a policy, and
           a window closed by its own key says so too, so the way back is a named key rather
           than a header arrow whose direction had to be guessed. Said beside the count, where
           the operator is looking when they open something. One line; the whole sentence is
           its title when it is cut, and it gives way before the window groups do. *@
        @if (Manager.LastEviction is { } eviction)
        {
            <div class="hdr__notice" role="status">
                <Glyph Icon="@Icons.Material.Filled.LayersClear" />
                <span class="hdr__notice-text" title="@eviction">@eviction</span>
                @if (Manager.CanGoBack)
                {
                    @* Back undoes the open that caused this, so the closed window returns where
                       it was. The key says which window that is: "Undo" alone left the
                       operator to guess. *@
                    <button class="hdr__notice-undo" type="button" title="Ctrl+Z"
                            aria-keyshortcuts="Control+Z Meta+Z" @onclick="GoBack">
                        @UndoLabel(Manager.LastEvictedTitles)
                    </button>
                }
                <button class="hdr__notice-dismiss" type="button" title="Dismiss" aria-label="Dismiss"
                        @onclick="Manager.ClearEviction">
                    <Glyph Icon="@Icons.Material.Filled.Close" />
                </button>
            </div>
        }

        @* The ceiling, and what the next open would cost. Was a note inside the
           launcher menu; the menu is gone, and this is where it was always
           really being asked. *@
        <span class="hdr__count num @(Manager.AtCapacity ? "hdr__count--full" : null)"
              title="@CapacityTip">
            @Manager.OpenCount/@Manager.Settings.MaxConcurrentWindows
        </span>
    </div>
</header>

@code {
    private RailMenu? _workspaces;

    // Owns its refresh: reads mutable manager state (open count, capacity), so it must
    // re-render whenever the manager changes rather than waiting on a parent to.
    protected override void OnInitialized() => Manager.Changed += OnChanged;

    private void OnChanged() => InvokeAsync(StateHasChanged);

    public void Dispose() => Manager.Changed -= OnChanged;

    /// <summary>
    /// Which window the next open would close. Said before the click rather than after, so
    /// eviction is never a surprise.
    /// </summary>
    private string? NextEviction => Manager.Windows
        .OrderByDescending(w => w.Minimised)
        .ThenBy(w => w.LastFocusedAt)
        .FirstOrDefault()?.Title;

    private string CapacityTip
        => Manager.AtCapacity && NextEviction is { } victim
            ? $"Desk is full at {Manager.Settings.MaxConcurrentWindows}. Opening another closes {victim} — least recently used."
            : $"{Manager.OpenCount} of {Manager.Settings.MaxConcurrentWindows} windows open.";


    private void Apply(Workspace workspace)
    {
        Manager.ApplyWorkspace(workspace);
        _workspaces?.Close();
    }

    /// <summary>
    /// The notice's Reopen key. Through the browser's history, like Ctrl+Z, so all of them walk
    /// one list (see Desk.OnUndoRequested). The step back puts a closed window in place and
    /// clears the notice itself.
    /// </summary>
    private async Task GoBack() => await JS.InvokeVoidAsync("history.back");

    /// <summary>
    /// The notice's key: "Reopen Journal" for one window, "Reopen 2 windows" for several. The
    /// manager keeps the titles beside its sentence (<see cref="WindowManager.LastEvictedTitles"/>),
    /// so the key names what it brings back rather than offering a bare "Undo" to decode.
    /// </summary>
    public static string UndoLabel(IReadOnlyList<string> titles) => titles.Count switch
    {
        0 => "Undo",
        1 => $"Reopen {titles[0]}",
        _ => $"Reopen {titles.Count} windows"
    };

    private void OpenSettings()
    {
        Desk.OpenSettings(Manager);
        _workspaces?.Close();
    }

    /// <summary>
    /// Close all goes through the same question the close key asks: a window holding unsaved
    /// input stops the lot, once, and only Discard empties the desk.
    /// </summary>
    private async Task CloseAll()
    {
        _workspaces?.Close();

        if (Manager.TryCloseAll())
            return;

        var discard = await Alerts.ConfirmAsync(
            "Discard unsaved changes and close every window?",
            message: "A window has input that has not been saved.",
            confirmLabel: "Discard",
            cancelLabel: "Keep editing",
            destructive: true);

        if (discard)
            Manager.CloseAll();
    }
}
